marcusd@lsl.co.uk writes:

>Theres a demo of a game called Myst kicking around the net, that has a
>quicktime movie showing how the game was put together, going from the designers
>story boards, to the graphics designers building the visuals, creating the
>sound effects, music, incorporating 'live' action and then finally putting the
>whole product together. Pretty impressive (and it was done with Hypercard???).
>I guess theres not really many one-man outfits around these days producing top
>quality games software (like Jeff Minter back in the good'ol days).

And yet, the development team for Myst was only about five or six people,
total, of which almost all the real development was done by two, and most
of that by one.  It's more difficult for one person to do an entire game
these days, especially the mega-monster-multimedia type games, but that
doesn't mean it's impossible.  There's a lot more kinds of games out there
than CD-ROM multimedia games, and you'll find that some of them are,
in fact, done by one person all by their lonesome.  Especially the
Tetris/Lemmings/Llamatron (hi, Jeff!)/Crystal Quest kind.

